const lessonDeepDives={
1:[
['The clock is a guide, not a threat',`Every day begins at 6:00am. The clock moves in ten-minute steps and the day ends at 2:00am, when your farmer passes out. That sounds strict at first, but a normal day is long enough for one main outing after your morning chores. Open menus pause time in single-player, so you can breathe, rearrange your bag, and decide what matters next.<ul><li>Choose one anchor task: mine, fish, forage, socialize, or reorganize.</li><li>Start heading home before you feel rushed; you do not earn a prize for using every minute.</li><li>Passing out usually costs money and may affect energy the next morning. It does not ruin the save.</li></ul>`],
['Energy, health, and food',`Energy powers most tool use. Health matters mainly in dangerous areas such as the Mines. Food may restore one or both, and some dishes add temporary buffs. Hover over food before eating so you can see exactly what it restores. A low-energy day can still include conversations, chest organization, shopping, and map exploration because walking itself uses no energy.`],
['Seasons, weather, and the TV',`Spring, Summer, Fall, and Winter each last 28 days. Most outdoor crops only survive in their listed season, so a crop must finish by day 28 unless it also grows in the next season. Each morning, the television can show tomorrow’s weather, the day’s luck, recipes, and beginner tips. Weather is useful for planning watering-can upgrades; luck can gently influence mining, treasure, and several chance-based activities, but a neutral day is still playable.`],
['Saving and replaying a day',`The game saves when you sleep and the overnight summary finishes. If you close the game before sleeping, that day can be replayed from the morning. Use that as a comfort tool, not a perfection rule. An imperfect purchase, missed fish, or inefficient route often becomes the memory that makes your farm feel personal.`],
['A gentle sample day for Spell Farm',`Try this order: check the TV, pet and feed your animals, collect products, handle crops, then choose one outing. Return with enough time to unload the bag and place tomorrow’s tools where you can see them. During Winter, replace crop watering with mines, fishing, friendships, upgrades, tree work, or Spring field planning. Your farm does not need to “win” the day.`]
],
2:[
['Learn the valley in small clusters',`Do not memorize every villager at once. Begin with the people you naturally meet around Pelican Town, then add the Mountain, Cindersap Forest, and Beach groups. A home is a reliable reference point even when a person’s schedule changes. Use the Villagers page in this site to tap a portrait, see a home, check a birthday, and review a few loved-gift ideas.`],
['Talking and weekly gifts',`A conversation is the easiest friendship action. Most villagers can receive up to two gifts per week, with no more than one gift per day. The Social tab shows who has already received gifts that week. You do not need to fill both gift slots for everyone; choosing two or three people you like is a perfectly good beginning.`],
['Birthdays make one gift count',`A birthday gift has a much larger friendship effect than an ordinary gift, which makes the calendar useful when you have limited items. A loved gift is wonderful, but a liked gift can still be thoughtful. Check the recipient before clicking—handing an item to the wrong nearby villager is surprisingly easy.`],
['Schedules, doors, and changing routines',`People move with the time, season, weather, weekday, and your story progress. Some rooms also require friendship before you can enter. If a door is locked or someone is missing, do not assume you did anything wrong: try their home in daylight, check the in-game map, or meet them another day.`],
['Friendship rewards without spoilers',`Friendship can lead to letters, recipes, new dialogue, and personal scenes. The site intentionally does not reveal those scenes or tell you whom to befriend. Let favorites emerge naturally. Keep a small gift chest near the farm exit, but only store items you are comfortable giving away.`]
],
3:[
['What Meadowlands gives you',`Meadowlands begins with a coop and two chickens, so animal care matters immediately. It also features blue grass, which animals especially enjoy. Treat natural pasture as a resource: clearing every patch may make the farm look tidy today but removes convenient grazing for later. Leave a generous area around the coop until you understand how much space your animals use.`],
['Build zones around your real route',`A practical first layout has five loose zones: morning care near the farmhouse, crops with future sprinkler room, pasture, machines and storage, and a renewable tree/resource area. These are guides rather than permanent borders. The best location is often the one you naturally walk past every day.`],
['Crop blocks and walking lanes',`Keep crop plots small enough to water without exhausting yourself. Quality Sprinklers eventually water the eight tiles surrounding them, so 3×3 modules with the sprinkler in the center are easy to expand. Trellis crops cannot be walked through; place them along an edge or leave access lanes. A scarecrow protects a broad circular area, so one can cover several early plots.`],
['Chests that answer questions',`Organize by the question you ask while playing: “Where are my seeds?”, “Where are my mine materials?”, “What can I gift?”, and “Which items am I keeping?” Color-coding helps. Place the chest where you use its contents rather than lining every chest against one distant wall.`],
['Season changes and flexible design',`Most outdoor crops disappear when their valid season ends, but tilled soil, paths, sprinklers, buildings, trees, and machines remain. Winter is ideal for moving chests, testing paths, marking building footprints, and preparing a Spring watering plan. You can move most farm buildings through Robin, so an early layout decision is rarely permanent.`]
],
4:[
['The daily animal loop',`Pet each animal once, make sure food is available, and collect products. In pleasant weather outside Winter, opening the animal door lets them eat grass outdoors. In Winter or bad weather, place hay on the feeding bench unless an upgraded building’s auto-feeder does it for you. Close doors if you enjoy the tidy routine, but animals safely return inside on their own in the evening.`],
['Hay math you can trust',`One coop or barn animal eats one hay on a day it cannot graze. A standard silo stores 240 hay. Two chickens therefore need 56 hay for 28 Winter days; your last reported 240 hay covered them comfortably, but a new cow raises the daily total to three. The dashboard and Hay Calculator use your editable current animal counts so the advice can change with your farm.`],
['Coop and barn progression',`A basic Coop or Barn houses four animals. Upgrades increase capacity and unlock new animal types or conveniences. Buy an upgrade because you want its capacity, incubator, auto-feeder, or animal options—not because the game demands a fixed sequence. Robin builds and upgrades the structure; Marnie sells most farm animals after the correct housing exists.`],
['Products become artisan goods',`Eggs can become mayonnaise, milk can become cheese, wool can become cloth, and truffles can become oil. A machine does not duplicate the product; it exchanges time for a more valuable or more useful item. Keep at least one raw product when you are still learning bundles, recipes, and gifts.`],
['Mood, warmth, and capacity',`Food, daily petting, shelter, and weather all affect animal mood. Put one heater in each occupied Coop or Barn for Winter; it supports mood but does not replace hay. Before buying an animal, confirm the building has an empty space and that your hay reserve can support the larger daily total.`]
],
5:[
['Match each tool to its job',`The Axe handles trees, branches, stumps, and logs. The Pickaxe breaks rocks, ore nodes, and placed flooring. The Hoe tills soil and uncovers artifact spots. The Watering Can waters crops. Scythes cut weeds, grass, and certain harvests without consuming energy. Fishing rods come from Willy and are replaced with better rods; Clint does not upgrade them.`],
['Tool tiers and charged swings',`Clint upgrades eligible farm tools in order: basic to Copper, Steel, Gold, then Iridium. Each step uses five matching bars plus gold. Upgraded Watering Cans and Hoes can affect larger areas when you hold the action button; upgraded Axes and Pickaxes break tougher obstacles and use fewer hits on ordinary ones.`],
['Exactly when an upgrade returns',`Hand one tool to Clint before he closes. You spend the rest of that day without it, wake the next morning without it, and collect it the following morning after the Blacksmith opens. Players call this a two-day upgrade. Check tomorrow’s weather before surrendering the Watering Can, or use Winter when outdoor crops are not your concern.`],
['Bars, coal, and the Furnace',`A standard Copper, Iron, Gold, or Iridium Bar uses five matching ore and one Coal. A Furnace uses 20 Copper Ore and 25 Stone to craft after you receive its recipe. Smelting times differ by material, so treat the Furnace as a background task: load it before chores or before bed, then collect the bar when ready. Coal is a repeated bottleneck—save some instead of turning every piece into a quick sale.`],
['Machines worth recognizing',`Your two Mayonnaise Machines turn eggs into mayonnaise. Preserves Jars process vegetables into pickles and fruit into jelly. Cheese Presses process milk; Tappers attach to mature trees; Recycling Machines transform some fishing trash; Kegs process several crops and animal products. You do not need every machine immediately. Build the one that matches products you reliably collect.`],
['Trees and Tappers',`Maple, Oak, and Pine trees produce different tapped goods. Tree seeds can grow into renewable wood lots when they have room. Avoid cutting every mature tree before deciding whether you want tapper lanes. Fruit Trees follow different placement rules and need the surrounding tiles clear while they grow.`],
['Quality Sprinklers for Spell Farm',`At Farming 6 you know the Quality Sprinkler recipe: one Iron Bar, one Gold Bar, and one Refined Quartz. Each waters the eight surrounding crop tiles every morning. Your last reported mine floor was 46, an excellent iron region; gold becomes common much deeper, beginning in the lava section around floor 80. Build a small field first instead of waiting for enough sprinklers to automate everything.`],
['A sensible first-Winter order',`Keep the Copper Axe if it is helping with wood and tougher stumps, aim for the next elevator checkpoints, gather Iron and Coal, and plan the Pickaxe upgrade around mining days. Save Refined Quartz and Gold Bars for sprinklers. The calculator page can total tool costs, bars, hay, and sprinkler materials before you spend anything.`]
],
6:[
['Read the fishing mini-game',`After a bite, keep the fish inside the green catch bar until the progress meter fills. Tapping or making short presses gives more control than holding continuously. The bar has momentum, so release slightly before it reaches the fish. A Training Rod makes early catches easier and is excellent practice even though it only catches basic fish.`],
['Rod, bait, and tackle are different',`A better rod does not automatically catch fish. The Fiberglass Rod can use bait, which reduces waiting time. The Iridium Rod can also use tackle, which changes the mini-game or adds a special benefit. Your permanent Fishing level and temporary food buffs enlarge the green bar; practice still matters more than equipment alone.`],
['Four questions for any fish',`Ask: which location, which season, what time, and what weather? Ocean, river, lake, forest pond, and special areas have different fish. If a fish refuses to appear, check all four conditions before blaming your skill. Begin with one calm favorite location instead of memorizing the entire fish list.`],
['Foraging as a route',`Pick up seasonal items while walking to another task. Chopping trees also earns Foraging experience, while the Axe consumes energy. Winter forage stands out clearly against snow, and artifact spots are easier to notice. Leave some trees and seeds in a dedicated wood lot so the farm keeps renewing its own materials.`],
['Wild Seeds and keeper rules',`Seasonal Wild Seeds grow into that season’s forage. Winter Seeds unlock at Foraging 7, which is your next milestone from the last reported level 6. Keep one unfamiliar fish or forage item until you check bundles, recipes, quests, and gifts; sell comfortable extras rather than filling every chest forever.`]
],
7:[
['Elevator checkpoints make the mine gentle',`The elevator permanently records every fifth floor. From your last reported floor 46, the next useful goal is floor 50—not “as deep as possible.” Start at 45, look for ladders, touch 50, and decide whether to leave. A saved checkpoint turns every later material run into a shorter trip.`],
['Where the common ores change',`Copper is common on floors 1–39, Iron on 40–79, and Gold on 80–119. Ore can appear outside its main region, and luck affects the day, but these bands are reliable planning shortcuts. Floor 46 puts Spell Farm in the frozen Iron region, which is exactly what Quality Sprinklers need.`],
['Pack a deliberate mine bag',`Bring Pickaxe, weapon, food, and enough empty slots for ore, coal, stone, and monster drops. Place the weapon beside the Pickaxe so one tap switches between them. Ladders matter more than breaking every rock. If the goal is Iron, do not spend half the day clearing Copper floors first.`],
['Combat positioning',`Weapons use no energy, but taking damage costs health. Fight in open tiles, keep enemies on one side, and use knockback to make space. Swords are forgiving, daggers are quick and close-range, and clubs hit slowly with strong knockback. You can replace early weapons through mine chests or the Adventurer’s Guild; Clint upgrades tools, not weapons.`],
['Leave before the trip becomes a loss',`Eat before health is critical, keep a route to the ladder, and leave when the clock, health, or supplies say “enough.” Reaching one checkpoint with a small bag of Iron is a successful trip. The Weapons page lists early replacements and clearly labels later forging information.`]
],
8:[
['The five skills and their XP',`Farming grows mainly through harvesting crops and animal care. Mining grows through breaking rocks and ore. Foraging grows through gathering and tree work. Fishing grows through catches and Crab Pots. Combat grows through defeating monsters. Swinging a tool without completing its related action usually does not award experience.`],
['What a level actually changes',`Skill levels improve related proficiency, which lowers energy use for relevant tools, and most levels unlock one or more recipes. Unlocks appear after you sleep. Farming 6 is why Spell Farm can already craft Quality Sprinklers even if the materials are still a project.`],
['Professions at levels 5 and 10',`Each skill offers a profession at level 5. That choice determines the two options offered at level 10. Read the exact benefits and choose for the activity you enjoy or want to encourage. A profit-maximizing choice is not automatically the best choice for your save.`],
['Temporary buffs versus permanent levels',`Food buffs temporarily raise a displayed skill, making related actions easier or more effective. A Fishing buff enlarges the catch bar; other skill buffs can improve proficiency or results. Buffs do not permanently add experience levels, and the clock icon beside a buff shows that it will wear off.`],
['Your current profile is a map',`The last report—Farming 6, Mining 4, Foraging 6, Fishing 2, Combat 2—shows a strong farming and foraging foundation, comfortable early mining, and two beginner skills that can grow gently. It is not a scorecard. The Skills page uses your editable current notes to suggest the next practical milestone in each area.`]
],
9:[
['Bundles are a guided tour',`The Community Center bundles introduce crops, forage, fish, minerals, artisan products, and town routines. Think of them as optional learning paths rather than chores. Completing a set helps the valley in practical ways, but this lesson leaves the specific rewards for you to discover.`],
['Read the slots before donating',`Open a bundle and count how many slots it actually requires. Some bundles show more possible items than the number needed. Once donated, an item stays there, so double-check that you are comfortable parting with its quality and quantity.`],
['Build one keeper chest',`Save one example of unfamiliar seasonal crops, forage, fish, artisan goods, minerals, and animal products until you check whether it has a bundle use. The inventory’s bundle indicator can help. A labeled chest near the farm exit makes this much easier than remembering everything.`],
['Plan by season, not by panic',`Notice which bundle items are seasonal and make a small list for the current month. If you miss something, the season returns next year and some items can appear through alternate sources. There is no requirement to finish the Community Center in Year 1.`],
['Choose gentle priorities',`Start with items already fitting your normal play: Spring crops while farming, frozen-floor minerals while mining, or easy river fish while practicing. A bundle should pull you toward a new activity with curiosity, not make every day feel scheduled.`]
],
10:[
['Separate the snapshot from today',`The 48,219g shown in the frozen card is your last reported checkpoint, not a live connection to the game. Update the Current Farm fields whenever your balance, animals, tools, skills, or next project change. Your daily planner and calculators read those current notes while preserving the original milestone.`],
['Keep a comfort reserve',`Before a large purchase, set aside enough for the next seed batch, animal hay, an expected tool upgrade, and one surprise you may genuinely want. The reserve is not wasted money; it protects your ability to change plans without grinding.`],
['Invest in friction reduction',`Ask which repeated task feels annoying. Sprinklers reduce watering, a stronger Pickaxe shortens mine trips, more storage shortens sorting, and a well-placed machine saves walking. The best purchase may create time and comfort rather than the highest theoretical gold-per-day.`],
['Use simple crop math',`For a rough decision, compare seed cost, number of harvests before day 28, and base sell value. Regrowing crops repay their seed over several harvests but occupy the tile all season. Single-harvest crops give more flexibility. The Crop Planner calculates harvest dates so late planting does not become an accidental loss.`],
['One-project Winter planning',`Pick one major project, one small comfort, and keep the rest flexible. For Spell Farm, a small Quality Sprinkler field can be the project, a tool upgrade or chest reorganization can be the comfort, and the remaining gold stays available for Spring discoveries.`]
],
11:[
['Use the two seasonal calendars',`The Villagers page now has a 28-day birthday calendar and a separate event calendar for every season. Festival cards show the event date, entry window, and location. Multi-day events mark each active day so you can see them at a glance.`],
['Festival time behaves in two ways',`Traditional festivals use a specific entry window and take over their location. Desert Festival, Trout Derby, SquidFest, and Night Market are different: normal time continues to pass while they are open. Finish animal care and urgent crops before a long trip, especially when the event is far from the farm.`],
['A low-pressure friendship routine',`Talk to people you naturally pass, remember a few favorite birthdays, and let relationships grow unevenly. Missing a day is normal. A small gift chest and the portrait directory are more useful than trying to memorize every loved item and schedule at once.`],
['Festival keepsakes can stay keepsakes',`Some festivals sell seeds, decorations, food, or rare items; others include games or contests. You never have to optimize every visit. If an item feels special, keeping it is a valid use even when it could be sold or gifted.`],
['Stay spoiler-light on purpose',`The event calendar tells you how to arrive without revealing cutscenes, contest outcomes, or character stories. Check the in-game invitation and calendar too: they are part of the experience and reflect what your save has unlocked.`]
],
12:[
['Design around the first hour',`Watch the route you naturally walk after waking: animals, machines, chests, crops, then the farm exit. Place the most frequent tasks along that loop. A layout that saves ten small detours every morning feels better than one that only looks symmetrical in a screenshot.`],
['Prototype footprints with paths',`Before asking Robin to build, mark the footprint and a one-tile walking border with inexpensive paths. Leave room for doors, gates, and animal movement. Buildings can usually be moved later, but planning the approach keeps the farm comfortable while it grows.`],
['Plan sprinkler modules and lanes',`Quality Sprinklers fit simple 3×3 modules, each watering eight crops. Group modules into fields with clear paths between them, and keep trellis crops on reachable edges. Start with the number of sprinklers you can craft now rather than designing an enormous field you cannot yet maintain.`],
['Give animals and machines their own rhythm',`Pasture needs grass, gates, and an easy path back to housing. Machines need access to the products they process and nearby storage for inputs and finished goods. A compact “morning workshop” can hold mayonnaise, cheese, and chest storage without turning the whole farm into a factory.`],
['Write a flexible Year 2 vision',`Choose three feelings—perhaps calm, magical, and lively—then list only a few supporting projects. Spell Farm might aim for a blue-grass meadow, a small sprinkler garden, a cozy animal lane, and a decorated corner for memories. Keep space for discoveries you have not met yet.`],
['Your next chapter',`A realistic first-Winter finish could be mine floor 50, a carefully timed Pickaxe upgrade, materials for a starter sprinkler field, and a refreshed Spring route. Stop when the plan feels exciting rather than exhausting. There is no single end-state called the perfect farm.`]
]
};

